    <heading level="1">11.2. Self-Check Exercises</heading>
    <exercise number="1" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Consider the sequence $$\left\{ \frac{n}{n^{2} + 1} \right\}$$ .</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Sketch the graph of the sequence.</item>
          <item>Show that the sequence is decreasing, that is, $$a_{1} &gt; a_{2} &gt; a_{3}\cdots$$ . Hint: Consider $$f{(x)} = x \slash (x^{2} + 1)$$ , and show that $$f$$ is decreasing by computing $$f^{\prime}$$ .</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>From the table,</paragraph>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>$$n$$</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>3</cell>
              <cell>4</cell>
              <cell>5</cell>
              <cell>⋯</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>$$f{(n)}$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{1}{2}$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{2}{5}$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{3}{10}$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{4}{17}$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\frac{5}{26}$$</cell>
              <cell>⋯</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <paragraph>we obtain the graph shown in the figure.</paragraph>
        <figure>
          <image alt="Details Five points are plotted in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. The horizontal axis n ranges from 0 to around 5 and the vertical axis y ranges from 0 to over 1/2. Points are plotted at (1, 1/2), (2, 2/5), (3, 3/10), (4, 4/17), and (5, 5/26)."/>
        </figure>
        <paragraph>We compute</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$f^{\prime}{(x)} = \frac{(x^{2} + 1){(1)} - x(2x)}{{(x^{2} + 1)}^{2}} = \frac{1 - x^{2}}{{(x^{2} + 1)}^{2}} &lt; 0$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>for $$x &gt; 1$$ , so $$f$$ is decreasing on $$(1,\infty)$$ . We conclude that $$\left\{ a_{n} \right\}$$ is decreasing.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="2" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Determine whether the sequence $$\left\{ \frac{3n^{2} + 2n + 1}{n^{2} + 4} \right\}$$ converges or diverges. If it converges, find its limit.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>We compute</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {{\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\frac{3n^{2} + 2n + 1}{n^{2} + 4}} = {\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\frac{3 + \frac{2}{n} + \frac{1}{n^{2}}}{1 + \frac{4}{n^{2}}}} { \text{Divide numerator and denominator by} n^{2}.}} \\ {\phantom{\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\frac{3n^{2} + 2n + 1}{n^{2} + 4}} = {\frac{3}{1} = 3}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
