    <exercise number="16" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Automobile MicroprocessorsOf the microprocessors manufactured by a microelectronics firm for use in regulating fuel consumption in automobiles, $$1\frac{1}{2}\%$$ are defective. It can be shown that the probability of getting at least one defective microprocessor in a random sample of $$n$$ microprocessors is $$f{(n)} = 1 - {(0.985)}^{n}$$ . Consider the sequence $$\left\{ a_{n} \right\}$$ defined by $$a_{n} = f{(n)}$$ .</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Write down the terms $$a_{1}$$ , $$a_{10}$$ , $$a_{100}$$ , and $$a_{1000}$$ of the sequence $$\left\{ a_{n} \right\}$$ .</item>
          <item>Evaluate $$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}a_{n}$$ , and interpret your results.</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$a_{1} = 0.015$$ , $$a_{10} \approx 0.14027$$ , $$a_{100} \approx 0.77939$$ , $$a_{1000} \approx 0.999999727$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$$1$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="17" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Savings AccountsAn amount of $$\$ 1000$$ is deposited in a bank that pays $$4\%{interest} \slash {year}$$ compounded daily (take the number of days in a year to be $$365$$ ). Let $$a_{n}$$ denote the total amount on deposit after $$n$$ days, assuming that no deposits or withdrawals are made during the period in question.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Find the formula for $$a_{n}$$ . Hint: See Section 5.3.</item>
          <item>Compute $$a_{1}$$ , $$a_{10}$$ , $$a_{50}$$ , and $$a_{100}$$ .</item>
          <item>What amount is on deposit after $$1$$ year?</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="18" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Accumulated AmountSuppose that $$\$ 100$$ is deposited into an account earning interest at $$6\% \slash {year}$$ compounded monthly. Let $$a_{n}$$ denote the amount on deposit (called the accumulated amount or the future value) at the end of the $$n$$ th month.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Show that $$a_{1} = 100(1.005)$$ , $$a_{2} = 100{(1.005)}^{2}$$ , and $$a_{3} = 100{(1.005)}^{3}$$ .</item>
          <item>Find the accumulated amount $$a_{n}$$ .</item>
          <item>Find the $$24$$ th term of the sequence $$\left\{ a_{n} \right\}$$ , and interpret your result.</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$a_{n} = 100{(1.005)}^{n}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$$a_{24} \approx 112.72$$ . The accumulated amount at the end of $$2$$ years is $$\$ 112.72$$ .</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="19" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Transmission of DiseaseIn the early stages of an epidemic, the number of persons who have contracted the disease on the $$(n + 1)$$ st day, $$a_{n + 1}$$ , is related to the number of persons who have the disease on the $$n$$ th day, $$a_{n}$$ , by the equation</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$a_{n + 1} = (1 + \textit{aN} - b)a_{n}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>where $$N$$ denotes the total population and $$a$$ and $$b$$ are positive constants that depend on the nature of the disease. Put $$r = 1 + \textit{aN} - b$$ .</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Write down the first $$n$$ terms of the sequence $$\left\{ a_{n} \right\}$$ .</item>
          <item>Evaluate $$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}a_{n}$$ for each of the three cases $$r &lt; 1$$ , $$r = 1$$ , and $$r &gt; 1$$ .</item>
          <item>Interpret the results of part (b).</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
